WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.780 --> 00:00:04.750
Sarah Hansen:
Today on the podcast, we're highlighting the

00:00:04.750 --> 00:00:10.200
power of open educational resources or OER.

00:00:10.200 --> 00:00:13.820
Elizabeth Siler:
I love using OER.

00:00:13.820 --> 00:00:18.910
One thing I love about teaching OER is that
I get to teach what I want to teach.

00:00:18.910 --> 00:00:22.960
What I think my students need to know, in
the way they need to learn it.

00:00:22.960 --> 00:00:27.880
Instead of just defaulting to what the textbooks
say.

00:00:27.880 --> 00:00:30.260
Sarah Hansen:
In this episode, I invited two instructors

00:00:30.270 --> 00:00:33.660
in to talk about open educational resources.

00:00:33.660 --> 00:00:38.359
One of them teaches negotiation and management
here at MIT and shares her course materials

00:00:38.359 --> 00:00:40.120
in MIT OpenCourseWare.

00:00:40.120 --> 00:00:47.579
The other recently adapted those materials
for use in her own course at another university.

00:00:47.579 --> 00:00:52.980
An organizational ombudsperson at MIT for
42 years, adjunct professor Mary Rowe was

00:00:52.980 --> 00:00:58.030
among the very first instructors to put her
course materials on OpenCourseWare almost

00:00:58.030 --> 00:00:59.540
20 years ago.

00:00:59.540 --> 00:01:05.390
That course is titled 15.667, Negotiation
and Conflict Management.

00:01:05.390 --> 00:01:10.439
Associate professor Elizabeth Siler has used
and adapted the materials from this course

00:01:10.439 --> 00:01:12.680
inner teachings at Worcester State University.

00:01:12.680 --> 00:01:18.900
When we brought these two instructors together,
something really special happened while much

00:01:18.900 --> 00:01:22.890
of the conversation did center on negotiation
itself.

00:01:22.890 --> 00:01:27.680
Mary and Elizabeth simultaneously began a
real time collaboration about how to teach

00:01:27.680 --> 00:01:31.460
negotiation with OER from Mary's course.

00:01:31.460 --> 00:01:33.810
Sarah Hansen:
The conversation brought forward so much of

00:01:33.810 --> 00:01:38.030
what we at OCW hope OER can do for educators.

00:01:38.030 --> 00:01:43.579
Things like creating community, shared ownership
of learning materials and fuel for inspired

00:01:43.579 --> 00:01:47.320
teaching, that we just really wanted to share
it with you.

00:01:47.320 --> 00:01:52.520
We hope it will help you find new inspiration
for using OER in your teaching.

00:01:52.520 --> 00:01:56.340
So without any further ado, let's jump into
the conversation.

00:01:56.340 --> 00:02:00.710
You'll hear me talking with Elizabeth first
and we start with one of my own insecurities

00:02:00.710 --> 00:02:05.460
about negotiation, an anxiety some of you
may share.

00:02:05.460 --> 00:02:08.119
When I think about negotiation, I like freeze
up.

00:02:08.119 --> 00:02:11.039
I'm like, Oh God, I have to negotiate a salary.

00:02:11.039 --> 00:02:12.760
I'm never going to get what I want.

00:02:12.760 --> 00:02:14.600
I can't ask for what I want.

00:02:14.600 --> 00:02:16.420
Elizabeth Siler:
Well, that's one of the reasons that I really

00:02:16.420 --> 00:02:19.261
like this course learning it and teaching
it.

00:02:19.261 --> 00:02:20.560
It's one of the things that...

00:02:20.560 --> 00:02:24.340
This is probably true for most students and
most people, they don't really like to deal

00:02:24.340 --> 00:02:31.620
with conflict, but learning about negotiation
as a way that you can learn that there are

00:02:31.620 --> 00:02:38.659
different ways to handle a conflict, or handle
a negotiation without changing your personality,

00:02:38.660 --> 00:02:39.300
right.

00:02:39.300 --> 00:02:41.440
Without being something that you're not.

00:02:41.440 --> 00:02:46.780
It's their skills and their behaviors, but
they're not personality traits.

00:02:46.790 --> 00:02:52.269
You can be shy and still be clear in what
you want and ask questions.

00:02:52.269 --> 00:02:55.780
You can still be a successful negotiator and
still not like speaking in public.

00:02:55.780 --> 00:02:59.069
Mary Rowe:
I think that's a wonderful example of how

00:02:59.069 --> 00:03:06.320
negotiation specialists try to build options,
which is that those who get into negotiations

00:03:06.320 --> 00:03:12.620
about anything, including bedtime for children,
want to come up with options.

00:03:12.620 --> 00:03:15.650
Sarah Hansen:
I'm taking that to heart as I think about

00:03:15.650 --> 00:03:19.140
my three-year-old tonight.

00:03:24.820 --> 00:03:32.240
So the course 15.667, the one we have on MIT,
OpenCourseWare it involves a number of case

00:03:32.250 --> 00:03:37.159
studies and simulations, which you've made
openly available Mary to the world.

00:03:37.159 --> 00:03:39.720
Tell us about the role of case studies in
the course?

00:03:39.720 --> 00:03:43.030
Mary Rowe:
So case studies and discussing real life is

00:03:43.030 --> 00:03:45.069
how you learn negotiations.

00:03:45.069 --> 00:03:46.420
It's absolutely essential.

00:03:46.420 --> 00:03:48.840
Elizabeth Siler:
There's one of them called dealing with an

00:03:48.840 --> 00:03:51.390
aggressive competitive negotiator.

00:03:51.390 --> 00:03:55.040
And this was a case, the main character's
named Mr. Canny.

00:03:55.040 --> 00:03:59.439
And he just wants what you're selling, but
he won't admit it and he won't give in.

00:03:59.439 --> 00:04:02.970
He won't, he keeps asking for more and it,
I mean, he's a real pain.

00:04:02.970 --> 00:04:07.599
But what I love about this is that the students
read this and they're like, "Oh, what do I

00:04:07.599 --> 00:04:08.689
do this person?

00:04:08.689 --> 00:04:09.950
How do I deal with this person?

00:04:09.950 --> 00:04:11.700
What's going on?"

00:04:11.700 --> 00:04:15.640
And then they talk about it with each other
and they start to see that, okay, there are

00:04:15.640 --> 00:04:16.739
some things we could do.

00:04:16.740 --> 00:04:20.200
And then we start to apply that process of,
well, what are his interests?

00:04:20.200 --> 00:04:22.199
What does he want?

00:04:22.199 --> 00:04:23.639
What's going on behind the scenes?

00:04:23.639 --> 00:04:27.110
Elizabeth Siler:
He's being very aggressive, but why is that?

00:04:27.110 --> 00:04:28.560
And what does he like?

00:04:28.560 --> 00:04:33.880
How might you talk to this person, so
he'll be more likely to take you seriously.

00:04:33.880 --> 00:04:39.160
And so the two things I like about this is,
one is that the teaching notes for some of

00:04:39.160 --> 00:04:44.570
these are really thorough and lovely and wonderful,
is this list that's like two pages long of

00:04:44.570 --> 00:04:48.090
things that you could do to address this situation.

00:04:48.090 --> 00:04:54.850
But the other thing about it is that this
is written so strongly that my students had

00:04:54.850 --> 00:04:57.480
some really visceral reactions to this.

00:04:57.480 --> 00:05:00.120
Some of them felt really angry.

00:05:00.120 --> 00:05:03.720
We did this at the end of the class, the end
of the semester, so that they were comfortable

00:05:03.720 --> 00:05:05.490
talking about...

00:05:05.490 --> 00:05:08.639
They were more comfortable with self disclosure
about how they were feeling and what went

00:05:08.639 --> 00:05:09.639
on.

00:05:09.639 --> 00:05:11.560
And some of them got really angry.

00:05:11.560 --> 00:05:15.650
Some of them got really scared.

00:05:15.650 --> 00:05:21.620
I was thrilled when this very articulate,
very kind of cool hockey player guy in my

00:05:21.620 --> 00:05:23.539
class said, "I'll admit it.

00:05:23.539 --> 00:05:26.639
You know, I was a little nervous when I was
reading this."

00:05:26.639 --> 00:05:28.139
Like, yes.

00:05:28.140 --> 00:05:30.640
Okay, good.

00:05:30.640 --> 00:05:32.800
Elizabeth Siler:
And so they could talk about how you have

00:05:32.800 --> 00:05:38.420
to deal with your own emotions right before
you can go on and negotiate, which in business,

00:05:38.420 --> 00:05:44.040
so much... emotions are treated like they are
bad, or that people don't have emotions.

00:05:44.050 --> 00:05:47.250
But you can't really negotiate well if you're
afraid.

00:05:47.250 --> 00:05:51.130
Or you can't negotiate well with somebody
if you're angry.

00:05:51.130 --> 00:05:55.080
So one of the things we talked about at the
end is, okay, here's how you felt about this.

00:05:55.080 --> 00:05:57.540
What are some things that you do?

00:05:57.540 --> 00:06:00.480
I mean, there was money involved in it, but
the thing that was really important about

00:06:00.490 --> 00:06:04.491
that one to me was to get the students to
acknowledge their emotions and figure out

00:06:04.491 --> 00:06:06.050
how to deal with them.

00:06:06.050 --> 00:06:10.370
And then to see that there's a whole lot more
things they could do in the situation than

00:06:10.370 --> 00:06:13.660
they thought of, than they had thought about
before.

00:06:13.660 --> 00:06:17.099
Mary Rowe:
It's hard to imagine, Elizabeth, more skillful

00:06:17.099 --> 00:06:21.290
discussion of teaching that case.

00:06:21.290 --> 00:06:25.370
Listening to you, I notice two things.

00:06:25.370 --> 00:06:32.470
One of them is that you've illuminated perfectly,
that we're all negotiating with ourselves

00:06:32.470 --> 00:06:38.539
and how we're dealing with our negotiation,
with our, our dealing, with our emotions.

00:06:38.539 --> 00:06:39.910
We've got two different points of view.

00:06:39.910 --> 00:06:44.259
I've got to make this work and I'm panicked
or I'm furious.

00:06:44.259 --> 00:06:52.840
So you're opening up a discussion at the end
of the class about even not just how I noticed

00:06:52.840 --> 00:06:56.169
that negotiations are important, I guess they
should have been.

00:06:56.169 --> 00:07:02.479
My first point is that emotions are important
that after all relationships between people,

00:07:02.479 --> 00:07:06.610
are almost always the most important thing
going on, even more so than money.

00:07:06.610 --> 00:07:12.949
But secondly, that in dealing with my emotions,
that I might be negotiating with myself, I

00:07:12.949 --> 00:07:14.860
loved your description.

00:07:14.860 --> 00:07:16.520
Elizabeth Siler:
And I love how you could tie that back to

00:07:16.520 --> 00:07:17.520
the theory.

00:07:17.520 --> 00:07:18.520
Right?

00:07:18.520 --> 00:07:20.030
negotiating with yourself because...

00:07:20.030 --> 00:07:25.180
Well, that's, frankly, that's one of the hardest
things I find about teaching is until I really,

00:07:25.180 --> 00:07:30.080
really know, like until I've taught the course
a lot or really know it, it takes me longer

00:07:30.080 --> 00:07:35.750
to make those connections from the student
experience to the theory.

00:07:35.750 --> 00:07:37.230
So it's really helpful to see that.

00:07:37.230 --> 00:07:39.330
Mary Rowe:
Well, and it's helpful to me to listen to

00:07:39.330 --> 00:07:41.169
you and see it.

00:07:41.169 --> 00:07:47.180
So always learning.

00:07:50.200 --> 00:07:58.760
Listening to Elizabeth again now reminded
me of how every student who plays has a chance

00:07:58.760 --> 00:08:06.520
to learn their own strengths and their own
vulnerabilities.

00:08:06.520 --> 00:08:10.040
And I think that could be elucidated better
in the teaching.

00:08:10.040 --> 00:08:14.379
I used to say at the beginning of the course,
that I have no idea whether we can teach people

00:08:14.379 --> 00:08:21.629
to negotiate better in the sense of getting
more of what you want.

00:08:21.629 --> 00:08:29.669
I was pretty sure that we can teach how you
and your team can lose less in working with

00:08:29.669 --> 00:08:31.550
other people.

00:08:31.550 --> 00:08:35.000
I'd like to think more about that in revising
the course.

00:08:35.000 --> 00:08:39.050
Mary Rowe:
We, haven't actually proven that we can teach

00:08:39.050 --> 00:08:44.930
people to negotiate better, in part because
how well you negotiate is for you to decide.

00:08:44.930 --> 00:08:50.100
And I can't tell you how you would think of
it.

00:08:50.100 --> 00:08:56.400
You might watch me negotiate and think I'd
given up the store and I'm pretty happy about

00:08:56.400 --> 00:09:00.260
it because I've maintained the relationships
that mattered to me.

00:09:00.260 --> 00:09:05.560
But I think we can teach people to defend
themselves better and that we can prove that.

00:09:05.560 --> 00:09:11.400
Anyway, you're right on the fringe know of
what I've been thinking about.

00:09:11.400 --> 00:09:14.700
Sarah Hansen:
The case studies and 15.667 are designed for

00:09:14.710 --> 00:09:19.100
graduate students in the MIT Sloan School
of Management.

00:09:19.100 --> 00:09:24.200
And many of these students come to the course
with previous management and business experience.

00:09:24.200 --> 00:09:27.610
Elizabeth Siler:
My students have not had the same kinds of

00:09:27.610 --> 00:09:29.670
work experience that a graduate student would
have.

00:09:29.670 --> 00:09:34.360
So I would add some things in to explain,
well, this is how a law firm works, or this

00:09:34.360 --> 00:09:35.980
is what a defense contractor does.

00:09:35.980 --> 00:09:37.920
And this is the way the organization is structured.

00:09:37.920 --> 00:09:39.340
So when you say this, this is what it means.

00:09:39.340 --> 00:09:40.920
It, when it says this, this is what it means.

00:09:40.920 --> 00:09:45.330
And it was just really helpful because that's
the stuff that they would need to know to

00:09:45.330 --> 00:09:48.690
make a decision, but they don't need to know
it to learn how to negotiate.

00:09:48.690 --> 00:09:54.650
I try to get the confusing things out of the
way so they can concentrate on what are the

00:09:54.650 --> 00:09:56.280
interests, and what am I going to say?

00:09:56.280 --> 00:09:57.640
Mary Rowe:
Very good ideas.

00:09:57.640 --> 00:10:00.520
Sarah Hansen:
It seems like you're touching on an important

00:10:00.520 --> 00:10:02.022
aspect of what it means to teach with OER.

00:10:02.022 --> 00:10:07.650
What it means to really discover something
and then to change it, to make it work for

00:10:07.650 --> 00:10:08.650
your students.

00:10:08.650 --> 00:10:09.650
Elizabeth Siler:
Yeah.

00:10:09.650 --> 00:10:13.160
I love using OER.

00:10:13.160 --> 00:10:18.140
One thing I love about teaching OER is that
I get to teach what I want to teach.

00:10:18.140 --> 00:10:22.200
What I think my students need to know in the
way they need to learn it.

00:10:22.200 --> 00:10:26.260
Instead of just defaulting to what the texts
books say.

00:10:26.260 --> 00:10:31.460
And sometimes that means using a textbook
that's an open resource and just adding things

00:10:31.460 --> 00:10:32.460
in.

00:10:32.460 --> 00:10:35.270
Sometimes it means pulling from more than
one textbook.

00:10:35.270 --> 00:10:37.510
Sometimes it means pulling things from...

00:10:37.510 --> 00:10:40.330
Writing things and pulling in podcasts and
articles and-

00:10:40.330 --> 00:10:42.080
Mary Rowe:
And the newspaper from yesterday.

00:10:42.080 --> 00:10:42.940
Elizabeth Siler:
... Newspaper from yesterday.

00:10:42.940 --> 00:10:45.180
Right.

00:10:45.180 --> 00:10:47.520
Sarah Hansen:
I asked Elizabeth if she had any tips for

00:10:47.520 --> 00:10:51.780
educators who wanted to begin teaching with
OER.

00:10:51.780 --> 00:10:55.080
Elizabeth Siler:
The first thing I would do is to talk to your

00:10:55.080 --> 00:11:03.300
librarian, because librarians are pretty much
at the forefront of open education resources.

00:11:03.300 --> 00:11:08.930
The next thing I would say is to find somebody
to work with find some community, which is,

00:11:08.930 --> 00:11:09.930
Mary's nodding.

00:11:09.930 --> 00:11:14.880
It's a lot easier if you were starting a course
kind of from scratch the way I was with this

00:11:14.880 --> 00:11:21.630
course to find somebody to work with, because
it helps split up the workload and it gives

00:11:21.630 --> 00:11:23.860
you somebody to bounce ideas off of.

00:11:23.860 --> 00:11:30.560
And the other thing is just to think about
what's working and what's not working about

00:11:30.560 --> 00:11:32.360
your current course.

00:11:32.360 --> 00:11:37.700
Because if something's not working, you can
probably find something else to replace it.

00:11:37.710 --> 00:11:42.690
It's not just about using a textbook that's
free, so that your students will have it.

00:11:42.690 --> 00:11:47.860
It's also about being able to teach what you
think they need to learn.

00:11:47.860 --> 00:11:50.920
Elizabeth Siler:
The other thing is, if you're using open educational

00:11:50.920 --> 00:11:53.730
resources that you can mix and match.

00:11:53.730 --> 00:11:59.460
There are several meta search engines for
OER materials right now.

00:11:59.460 --> 00:12:03.960
And the good news is the bad news that there
are so many of them, you might have to dig

00:12:03.960 --> 00:12:05.670
through to find what you're looking for.

00:12:05.670 --> 00:12:10.400
But the good news is that there is a lot out
there.

00:12:10.400 --> 00:12:12.400
And if there's not, then you can write it.

00:12:12.400 --> 00:12:16.630
Sarah Hansen:
Elizabeth, what plans do you have to share

00:12:16.630 --> 00:12:22.100
the materials you've adapted from Mary's course
with others?

00:12:22.100 --> 00:12:23.700
Elizabeth Siler:
Right.

00:12:23.700 --> 00:12:29.490
That's part of open education resources overall
is community again, and it's doing what you

00:12:29.490 --> 00:12:31.880
have and sharing it with other people.

00:12:31.880 --> 00:12:36.350
Once I finish, I'm probably going to work
with the instructional designer at my school,

00:12:36.350 --> 00:12:40.110
actually to put things in a word document
and PDF documents.

00:12:40.110 --> 00:12:45.890
So that they could be in a format that can
be easily adapted by other professors and

00:12:45.890 --> 00:12:49.740
then find a platform for it.

00:12:49.740 --> 00:12:52.720
Sarah Hansen:
Before we ended our conversation, I asked

00:12:52.730 --> 00:12:57.070
Mary and Elizabeth, if there was anything
else, they wanted to ask each other about

00:12:57.070 --> 00:12:59.960
teaching with the OER in 15.667.

00:12:59.960 --> 00:13:01.360
Mary Rowe:

00:13:01.360 --> 00:13:06.420
Well I would like to ask, Elizabeth, did you
use the task of writing a perceived injurious

00:13:06.430 --> 00:13:07.430
experience letter?

00:13:07.430 --> 00:13:09.230
Elizabeth Siler:
Oh, yes I did.

00:13:09.230 --> 00:13:12.630
Mary Rowe:
The reason I ask is that of everything that

00:13:12.630 --> 00:13:17.270
I ever stumbled into, of course, everything
you've heard from me is hindsight.

00:13:17.270 --> 00:13:25.480
I never had the wit to plan it, but of everything
that I ever came up with in teaching negotiations,

00:13:25.480 --> 00:13:31.410
the perceived injurious letter and various
variations of it, flew by far the furthest.

00:13:31.410 --> 00:13:38.131
I would get requests, I remember one from
Canada to reproduce the instructions in 500,000

00:13:38.131 --> 00:13:43.710
copies for school, for elementary school or
something.

00:13:43.710 --> 00:13:51.430
And still on my website at Sloan, it's now
called ideas to consider if you've been harassed,

00:13:51.430 --> 00:13:52.520
right.

00:13:52.520 --> 00:13:58.330
But basically the notion of how to deal with
a situation where you have felt injured or

00:13:58.330 --> 00:14:00.040
hurt.

00:14:00.040 --> 00:14:02.620
So I'm very interested in your experience
with it.

00:14:02.620 --> 00:14:03.620
Elizabeth Siler:
Yeah.

00:14:03.620 --> 00:14:07.770
From one of the last assignments in this semester,
the students could write the perceived injurious-

00:14:07.770 --> 00:14:08.850
Mary Rowe:
Experience.

00:14:08.850 --> 00:14:10.300
Elizabeth Siler:
... experience, right.

00:14:10.300 --> 00:14:14.330
They could write a PIE letter or they could
write an apology letter.

00:14:14.330 --> 00:14:17.710
They could choose, and they could write about
something from themselves and their own life.

00:14:17.710 --> 00:14:21.540
Or they could write about something from one
of the cases, because not everybody has the

00:14:21.540 --> 00:14:24.100
same level of comfort and disclosing and that's
fine.

00:14:24.100 --> 00:14:26.170
They can still learn.

00:14:26.170 --> 00:14:33.900
And what I found out was, first of all I was
really surprised still, even with being around

00:14:33.900 --> 00:14:38.820
hundreds of undergraduates every day, just
how much, how personal people were willing

00:14:38.820 --> 00:14:40.270
to get at this.

00:14:40.270 --> 00:14:43.800
I mean, it was clear I was the only person
who was going to read this.

00:14:43.800 --> 00:14:44.850
Nobody was ever going to do it.

00:14:44.850 --> 00:14:46.520
They didn't have to send it.

00:14:46.520 --> 00:14:50.140
But people got really personal and I thought
they would be getting personal more about

00:14:50.140 --> 00:14:55.210
how they'd been injured, but they got really
personal and really explicit and really thoughtful

00:14:55.210 --> 00:14:56.900
about how they had hurt other people.

00:14:56.900 --> 00:15:00.420
Elizabeth Siler:
And that was really powerful.

00:15:00.420 --> 00:15:06.090
I mean, I was kind of blown away by how willing
they were to take this on and the things they

00:15:06.090 --> 00:15:07.090
said.

00:15:07.090 --> 00:15:12.100
And I wish I had done it earlier in the semester
so that I could find out some more, how did

00:15:12.100 --> 00:15:13.320
they react to that?

00:15:13.320 --> 00:15:15.220
Like what did they get out of it?

00:15:15.220 --> 00:15:18.591
Going back to the beginning of this, I think
that maybe why this spoke to me so much, it's

00:15:18.591 --> 00:15:22.380
because it was everything every day.

00:15:22.380 --> 00:15:28.050
And that there are a lot of things you can
do in business classes with a very low amount

00:15:28.050 --> 00:15:34.200
of emotions involved, and that's great, but
this isn't one of them.

00:15:34.200 --> 00:15:38.990
And we don't really talk about it much in
any other business classes, but we're human.

00:15:38.990 --> 00:15:43.230
As my finance professor said, Annabel Burrell.

00:15:43.230 --> 00:15:46.000
And she said, we are more than rational beings.

00:15:46.000 --> 00:15:47.000
Right?

00:15:47.000 --> 00:15:50.810
And I think this is one of the places where
that's incredibly clear.

00:15:50.810 --> 00:15:53.950
Mary Rowe:
You remind me also that it's the basis for

00:15:53.950 --> 00:15:55.980
leadership.

00:15:55.980 --> 00:16:01.980
To the extent that management training includes
a discussion of leadership, emotions had relationships

00:16:01.980 --> 00:16:04.560
are profoundly fundamental.

00:16:04.560 --> 00:16:08.170
Sarah Hansen:
You two have completely changed my perspective

00:16:08.170 --> 00:16:10.660
on what negotiation is.

00:16:10.660 --> 00:16:16.200
I feel like you've created a pathway for me
to explore this area more.

00:16:16.200 --> 00:16:20.240
I feel like it's accessible to me that I might
actually be good at it.

00:16:20.250 --> 00:16:21.250
Mary Rowe:
Oh, yeah.

00:16:21.250 --> 00:16:23.520
Sarah Hansen:
That I might be doing it already without knowing

00:16:23.520 --> 00:16:27.000
it.

00:16:27.000 --> 00:16:32.120
If you're getting the same feeling that maybe
you or your students might already be, or

00:16:32.120 --> 00:16:37.580
it could become good at negotiation with the
right tools, and want to learn more, you can

00:16:37.580 --> 00:16:44.060
explore, download, reuse, and remix all of
Mary's teaching materials from our MIT OpenCourseWare

00:16:44.060 --> 00:16:45.320
website.

00:16:45.320 --> 00:16:49.550
On our website and in the show notes, you'll
find the case study about dealing with an

00:16:49.550 --> 00:16:55.300
aggressive competitive negotiator, like Mr.
Canny and guidelines for writing a perceived

00:16:55.300 --> 00:16:57.480
injurious experience letter.

00:16:57.480 --> 00:17:02.430
If you, like Elizabeth use these or other
OER from OpenCourseWare in your teaching,

00:17:02.430 --> 00:17:07.390
we'd love to hear about it because when you
share your story of using OER, you inspire

00:17:07.390 --> 00:17:08.630
others.

00:17:08.630 --> 00:17:10.720
And that's what we're all about.

00:17:10.720 --> 00:17:12.020
Thank you for listening.

00:17:12.020 --> 00:17:15.530
And we hope you enjoyed season one of Chalk
Radio.

00:17:15.530 --> 00:17:18.300
We're already hard at work on season two.

00:17:18.300 --> 00:17:22.520
If there's a professor you'd like to hear
from reach out and let us know.

00:17:22.520 --> 00:17:27.100
Until next time I'm Sarah Hansen from MIT,
